<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
================================================================================
 DWSIM FLOWSHEET — MIXER + HEATER, ETHANOL/WATER
================================================================================

 WHAT THIS FILE IS
 =================
 A minimal, heavily annotated DWSIM flowsheet in DWSIM's uncompressed XML
 format (.dwxml). Two feed streams are mixed and the combined stream is heated.

 It exists to show you the *shape* of a DWSIM save file: where compounds,
 property packages, unit operations, connections, and stream conditions live,
 and therefore what a `git diff` of a real simulation will show you.

 HONEST CAVEAT — READ THIS BEFORE YOU DOUBLE-CLICK IT
 ====================================================
 This is a hand-written teaching skeleton, not a file exported by DWSIM. The
 real schema is larger and version-dependent: DWSIM writes many more property
 fields per object, and the exact element names shift between major versions.
 Opening this directly may fail or produce a flowsheet with missing properties.

 To get a file that definitely loads, build the same flowsheet in DWSIM once
 (it takes about five minutes — instructions below) and save as .dwxml. Then
 diff yours against this one to see what a production file carries.

 THE POINT, WHICH SURVIVES THE CAVEAT
 =====================================
 DWSIM's native format is XML. Aspen's is binary. That difference means:

   - `git diff` shows you that someone changed the heater outlet from 80 to
     95 degC, in plain text, with no licence required to read it
   - code review of a simulation change is possible
   - two people can work on different parts of a flowsheet and merge, provided
     they touched different objects
   - the file is readable in fifty years

 DWSIM also writes .dwxmz, which is exactly this XML inside a zip container.
 Commit .dwxml, not .dwxmz, when you want diffs. The size penalty is small and
 the review benefit is large. Set it in Tools -> General Settings -> Save file
 format, or just choose the format in the Save dialog.

 BUILDING THIS IN DWSIM YOURSELF
 ===============================
   1. New steady-state simulation
   2. Compounds: add Water and Ethanol
   3. Property package: NRTL  (Peng-Robinson is wrong for this pair —
      ethanol/water is strongly non-ideal and needs an activity model)
   4. Drag on: two Material Streams, a Mixer, a Heater, two more Material
      Streams for the mixer outlet and heater outlet, and an Energy Stream
   5. Connect: S-01 + S-02 -> MIX-100 -> S-03 -> HEAT-100 -> S-04,
      with E-01 into HEAT-100
   6. Set the feed conditions to match the values below
   7. Set the heater outlet temperature to 353.15 K
   8. Run. The heater duty should come out around 250-300 kW.
   9. Save As -> .dwxml, and diff it against this file

 UNITS
 =====
 Everything below is SI base: K, Pa, kg/s, W. DWSIM stores SI internally
 regardless of the units you display, which is one fewer thing to get wrong
 when reading a file by hand.

 Licence: MIT. Part of github.com/open-cheme-hub/templates
================================================================================
-->
<DWSIM_Simulation_Data>

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       GENERAL INFORMATION
       Descriptive metadata. Worth filling in properly: this is what a reviewer
       reads first, and "Simulation1" tells them nothing.
       ========================================================================== -->
  <GeneralInfo>
    <Title>Ethanol-Water Mixer and Heater — Open ChemE Hub template</Title>
    <Author>Open ChemE Hub contributors</Author>
    <Comments>
      Teaching example: two feeds mixed and heated to 80 degC.
      Property package NRTL — ethanol/water is strongly non-ideal and a cubic
      EOS will give you the wrong VLE. Change this only with a reason you can
      write down.
      Basis: 1 atm, steady state, no reaction.
    </Comments>
    <Created>2026-01-15T09:00:00</Created>
    <LastModified>2026-01-15T09:00:00</LastModified>
    <DWSIMVersion>8.7.0</DWSIMVersion>
  </GeneralInfo>

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       COMPOUNDS
       The compound list is the first thing to check when a flowsheet gives an
       unexpected answer. A compound present but not in the property package's
       binary parameter set will silently behave as ideal.
       ========================================================================== -->
  <Compounds>
    <Compound>
      <Name>Water</Name>
      <CAS_Number>7732-18-5</CAS_Number>
      <Formula>H2O</Formula>
      <Molar_Weight>18.0153</Molar_Weight>
      <Critical_Temperature>647.13</Critical_Temperature>
      <Critical_Pressure>21940000</Critical_Pressure>
      <Acentric_Factor>0.3449</Acentric_Factor>
      <Database>ChemSep</Database>
    </Compound>
    <Compound>
      <Name>Ethanol</Name>
      <CAS_Number>64-17-5</CAS_Number>
      <Formula>C2H6O</Formula>
      <Molar_Weight>46.0690</Molar_Weight>
      <Critical_Temperature>513.92</Critical_Temperature>
      <Critical_Pressure>6148000</Critical_Pressure>
      <Acentric_Factor>0.6436</Acentric_Factor>
      <Database>ChemSep</Database>
    </Compound>
  </Compounds>

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       PROPERTY PACKAGES
       The single most consequential choice in the file, and the one most often
       left at the default. NRTL with fitted binaries reproduces the 95.6 wt%
       ethanol azeotrope at 1 atm; Peng-Robinson does not.
       ========================================================================== -->
  <PropertyPackages>
    <PropertyPackage>
      <ID>PP-001</ID>
      <Name>NRTL</Name>
      <Type>NRTLPropertyPackage</Type>
      <IsDefault>True</IsDefault>
      <VaporPhaseModel>Ideal</VaporPhaseModel>
      <LiquidDensityModel>Rackett</LiquidDensityModel>
      <EnthalpyModel>ExcessEnthalpy</EnthalpyModel>
      <BinaryInteractionParameters>
        <!-- NRTL parameters for ethanol(1)/water(2), from the DWSIM/ChemSep
             database regression. Aij in cal/mol, alpha dimensionless.
             If you regress your own, record where the VLE data came from —
             a binary parameter with no provenance is a guess with a decimal
             point. -->
        <Pair>
          <Component1>Ethanol</Component1>
          <Component2>Water</Component2>
          <A12>-55.1681</A12>
          <A21>670.4442</A21>
          <alpha12>0.3031</alpha12>
        </Pair>
      </BinaryInteractionParameters>
    </PropertyPackage>
  </PropertyPackages>

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       FLOWSHEET OBJECTS
       Each object carries a Name (the tag you see on the canvas), a Type, and
       canvas coordinates. Coordinates change whenever anyone nudges an icon,
       which is why a diff of a real DWSIM file is noisier than it should be —
       expect position-only changes and review past them.
       ========================================================================== -->
  <SimulationObjects>

    <!-- ================ FEED STREAM 1: aqueous ethanol =====================
         1.0 kg/s at 25 degC, 1 atm, 20 mol% ethanol.
         Note mole fractions, not mass. DWSIM stores mole fractions internally;
         confusing the two is the classic first mistake with this file format. -->
    <SimulationObject>
      <Name>S-01</Name>
      <Tag>Feed A — dilute ethanol</Tag>
      <Type>DWSIM.Thermodynamics.Streams.MaterialStream</Type>
      <ObjectType>MaterialStream</ObjectType>
      <X>60</X><Y>120</Y>
      <PropertyPackage>PP-001</PropertyPackage>
      <Phases>
        <Phase>
          <Name>Mixture</Name>
          <Properties>
            <temperature>298.15</temperature>          <!-- K   (25 degC) -->
            <pressure>101325</pressure>                <!-- Pa  (1 atm)   -->
            <massflow>1.0</massflow>                   <!-- kg/s          -->
            <molarfraction_Ethanol>0.20</molarfraction_Ethanol>
            <molarfraction_Water>0.80</molarfraction_Water>
          </Properties>
        </Phase>
      </Phases>
      <SpecificationType>Temperature_and_Pressure</SpecificationType>
    </SimulationObject>

    <!-- ================ FEED STREAM 2: warm water ==========================
         0.5 kg/s at 60 degC, 1 atm, pure water. -->
    <SimulationObject>
      <Name>S-02</Name>
      <Tag>Feed B — warm water</Tag>
      <Type>DWSIM.Thermodynamics.Streams.MaterialStream</Type>
      <ObjectType>MaterialStream</ObjectType>
      <X>60</X><Y>240</Y>
      <PropertyPackage>PP-001</PropertyPackage>
      <Phases>
        <Phase>
          <Name>Mixture</Name>
          <Properties>
            <temperature>333.15</temperature>          <!-- K   (60 degC) -->
            <pressure>101325</pressure>
            <massflow>0.5</massflow>
            <molarfraction_Ethanol>0.00</molarfraction_Ethanol>
            <molarfraction_Water>1.00</molarfraction_Water>
          </Properties>
        </Phase>
      </Phases>
      <SpecificationType>Temperature_and_Pressure</SpecificationType>
    </SimulationObject>

    <!-- ================ MIXER ==============================================
         PressureCalculation Minimum: outlet takes the lowest inlet pressure.
         The alternatives are Maximum and Average, and picking the wrong one is
         a quiet way to gain pressure you don't have. -->
    <SimulationObject>
      <Name>MIX-100</Name>
      <Tag>Feed mixer</Tag>
      <Type>DWSIM.UnitOperations.UnitOperations.Mixer</Type>
      <ObjectType>Mixer</ObjectType>
      <X>200</X><Y>180</Y>
      <Properties>
        <PressureCalculation>Minimum</PressureCalculation>
      </Properties>
    </SimulationObject>

    <!-- ================ MIXED STREAM =======================================
         Calculated, not specified. Values here are results and are overwritten
         on every solve — do not hand-edit them, and don't be surprised when
         they show up in a diff after a run. -->
    <SimulationObject>
      <Name>S-03</Name>
      <Tag>Mixer outlet</Tag>
      <Type>DWSIM.Thermodynamics.Streams.MaterialStream</Type>
      <ObjectType>MaterialStream</ObjectType>
      <X>320</X><Y>180</Y>
      <PropertyPackage>PP-001</PropertyPackage>
      <Phases>
        <Phase>
          <Name>Mixture</Name>
          <Properties>
            <temperature>309.4</temperature>           <!-- calculated ~36 degC -->
            <pressure>101325</pressure>
            <massflow>1.5</massflow>                   <!-- 1.0 + 0.5, by mass balance -->
          </Properties>
        </Phase>
      </Phases>
      <SpecificationType>Calculated</SpecificationType>
    </SimulationObject>

    <!-- ================ HEATER =============================================
         Specified by outlet temperature; duty is then calculated. The other
         way round (specify duty, calculate temperature) is what you want when
         modelling a real exchanger against a fixed utility. -->
    <SimulationObject>
      <Name>HEAT-100</Name>
      <Tag>Preheater</Tag>
      <Type>DWSIM.UnitOperations.UnitOperations.Heater</Type>
      <ObjectType>Heater</ObjectType>
      <X>440</X><Y>180</Y>
      <Properties>
        <CalcMode>OutletTemperature</CalcMode>
        <OutletTemperature>353.15</OutletTemperature>  <!-- K (80 degC) -->
        <PressureDrop>0</PressureDrop>                 <!-- Pa — a real exchanger has one -->
        <Efficiency>100</Efficiency>                   <!-- % of duty reaching the process -->
      </Properties>
    </SimulationObject>

    <!-- ================ ENERGY STREAM ======================================
         Carries the heater duty. Calculated here because the heater is
         temperature-specified. Roughly 250-300 kW for this feed. -->
    <SimulationObject>
      <Name>E-01</Name>
      <Tag>Heater duty</Tag>
      <Type>DWSIM.Thermodynamics.Streams.EnergyStream</Type>
      <ObjectType>EnergyStream</ObjectType>
      <X>440</X><Y>300</Y>
      <Properties>
        <EnergyFlow>0</EnergyFlow>                     <!-- W, calculated on solve -->
      </Properties>
    </SimulationObject>

    <!-- ================ PRODUCT STREAM =====================================
         At 80 degC and 1 atm this is still single-phase liquid: the bubble
         point of a 20 mol% ethanol mixture at 1 atm is around 82 degC. Push the
         heater to 90 degC and you get two phases, and every downstream unit
         behaves differently. Worth trying, to see it happen. -->
    <SimulationObject>
      <Name>S-04</Name>
      <Tag>Heated product</Tag>
      <Type>DWSIM.Thermodynamics.Streams.MaterialStream</Type>
      <ObjectType>MaterialStream</ObjectType>
      <X>560</X><Y>180</Y>
      <PropertyPackage>PP-001</PropertyPackage>
      <Phases>
        <Phase>
          <Name>Mixture</Name>
          <Properties>
            <temperature>353.15</temperature>
            <pressure>101325</pressure>
            <massflow>1.5</massflow>
          </Properties>
        </Phase>
      </Phases>
      <SpecificationType>Calculated</SpecificationType>
    </SimulationObject>

  </SimulationObjects>

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       CONNECTIONS
       The topology. This section is the one you actually want to read in a
       diff: a changed connection is a changed flowsheet, whereas a changed
       coordinate is somebody tidying the canvas.
       ========================================================================== -->
  <Connections>
    <Connection>
      <From>S-01</From><FromPort>Outlet</FromPort>
      <To>MIX-100</To><ToPort>Inlet1</ToPort>
    </Connection>
    <Connection>
      <From>S-02</From><FromPort>Outlet</FromPort>
      <To>MIX-100</To><ToPort>Inlet2</ToPort>
    </Connection>
    <Connection>
      <From>MIX-100</From><FromPort>Outlet</FromPort>
      <To>S-03</To><ToPort>Inlet</ToPort>
    </Connection>
    <Connection>
      <From>S-03</From><FromPort>Outlet</FromPort>
      <To>HEAT-100</To><ToPort>Inlet</ToPort>
    </Connection>
    <Connection>
      <From>E-01</From><FromPort>Outlet</FromPort>
      <To>HEAT-100</To><ToPort>EnergyInlet</ToPort>
    </Connection>
    <Connection>
      <From>HEAT-100</From><FromPort>Outlet</FromPort>
      <To>S-04</To><ToPort>Inlet</ToPort>
    </Connection>
  </Connections>

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       SOLVER SETTINGS
       Loosening tolerance to force convergence is a way of hiding a problem
       rather than solving one. If a flowsheet only converges at 1e-3, find out
       why before you trust the numbers.
       ========================================================================== -->
  <SolverSettings>
    <SolverMode>SimultaneousAdjustSolver</SolverMode>
    <MassBalanceTolerance>0.0001</MassBalanceTolerance>
    <EnergyBalanceTolerance>0.0001</EnergyBalanceTolerance>
    <MaximumIterations>100</MaximumIterations>
    <SimultaneousAdjust>False</SimultaneousAdjust>
  </SolverSettings>

  <!-- ==========================================================================
       EXERCISES

       1. Raise the heater outlet to 363.15 K (90 degC) and observe the vapour
          fraction in S-04 go non-zero. Note how much the duty jumps — that step
          is the latent heat, and it is why utility sizing is sensitive to a few
          degrees near the bubble point.

       2. Switch the property package to Peng-Robinson and compare the bubble
          point. The disagreement with NRTL is the reason activity models exist,
          and it is a more convincing argument than any textbook paragraph.

       3. Add a Flash unit downstream of S-04 and separate the phases. Check
          whether the vapour composition approaches the azeotrope.

       4. Commit each change as a separate commit and read the diffs. That is
          the actual lesson of this file.
       ========================================================================== -->

</DWSIM_Simulation_Data>
